],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,49454,323,14927,1817,1744,315,264,79151,47311,13,2014,862,12761,11,2176,29136,28370,1191,400,87,61,17,10,19,87,41715,4385,2412,12947,576,11341,315,279,49319,517,11,400,65,61,17,12,19,580,54876,315,16247,594,47311,311,279,49319,517,315,14927,594,47311,374,6144,311,279,11341,315,14927,594,6783,4647,311,16247,594,6783,4647,13,1416,862,6783,3793,525,537,6144,11,1477,279,2629,315,279,6783,3793,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nEric and Charles each think of a quadratic polynomial. To their surprise, both quadratics start $x^2+4x+\\cdots$. The ratio of the discriminant, $b^2-4ac$, of Eric's polynomial to the discriminant of Charles's polynomial is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term. If their constant terms are not equal, find the sum of the constant terms.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"54ed9d3dc559964fe71eb98c1e0fc2f66cd8d899a9b66bfe4ebf42deac99ab59"},"seconds":25.68614935874939,"seed":537207642342662529,"stop":"eos","text":"Let Eric's quadratic polynomial be \\(x^2 + 4x + a\\) and Charles's quadratic polynomial be \\(x^2 + 4x + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the constant terms of their respective polynomials. The discriminant of a quadratic polynomial \\(x^2 + 4x + c\\) is given by \\(4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot c = 16 - 4c\\).\n\nThe discriminant of Eric's polynomial is \\(16 - 4a\\) and the discriminant of Charles's polynomial is \\(16 - 4b\\). According to the problem, the ratio of the discriminants is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term. This can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side of the equation, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4(4 - a)}{4(4 - b)} = \\frac{4 - a}{4 - b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to eliminate the fractions, we get:\n\n\\[\na(4 - a) = b(4 - b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n4a - a^2 = 4b - b^2\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\na^2 - b^2 = 4a - 4b\n\\]\n\nFactoring the left side and the right side, we have:\n\n\\[\n(a - b)(a + b) = 4(a - b)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a \\neq b\\), we can divide both sides by \\(a - b\\):\n\n\\[\na + b = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the constant terms is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
